\title{Spectral Line-Locking in Fixed-Amplitude \\ Complex-Weighted Undirected Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study a complex-weighted variant of the Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi random graph in which every present edge of an undirected connection pattern carries a single common complex amplitude $z$. The weighted adjacency matrix then factors as $A = z\,B$, where $B$ is the ordinary real symmetric zero-one indicator matrix of the edge relation. We prove that this factorization forces the spectrum to be one-dimensional: for a symmetric edge relation and any nonzero amplitude $z$, every eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $A$ satisfies $\lambda = z\mu$ for some real number $\mu$, so the entire spectrum collapses onto the complex line $\R\cdot z$. This is the exact and sharpest obstruction to a circular-law (disk-filling) limiting spectral distribution. The engine of the result is an elementary Rayleigh-quotient reality theorem, applied to abstract eigenpairs rather than only spectral-theorem eigenvalues. We complement line-locking with the global multiplicative invariants of the model---the determinant scales as $z^n\det(B)$ and the loopless trace vanishes---and with a complete-graph analysis showing that the mean-direction eigenvalue $(n-1)z$ escapes the heuristic radius $\sqrt{n}\,|z|$ for every order $n \ge 3$, with the threshold beginning sharply at $n=3$. We discuss why genuine two-dimensional spectra require breaking the scalar--Hermitian factorization through independently phased or directed edges.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi random graph $G(n,p)$ places $n$ vertices and includes each undirected edge independently with probability $p$. Its spectral theory is a cornerstone of probabilistic combinatorics: the adjacency matrix is real symmetric, its empirical spectral distribution converges to Wigner's semicircle law after centering and scaling, and the top eigenvalue separates as a mean-degree outlier.

A natural generalization replaces the \emph{probability} $p$ by a \emph{complex amplitude} $z$: rather than recording an edge as present ($1$) or absent ($0$), one records each present edge with a complex weight $z = re^{i\theta}$. Because complex, non-Hermitian random matrices are the natural habitat of the \emph{circular law}---where the empirical spectral distribution fills a two-dimensional disk---one might expect the complex-weighted graph to exhibit disk-filling behavior. We show that for the \emph{fixed-amplitude undirected} model this expectation is exactly wrong, and we isolate precisely why.

The central structural fact is that when every present edge carries the \emph{same} amplitude $z$, the weighted adjacency matrix factors as a scalar multiple of a real symmetric matrix:
\[
A = z\,B, \qquad B_{ij} \in \{0,1\}, \quad B = B^{\mathsf T}.
\]
All randomness and graph structure reside in $B$; the amplitude $z$ is a single global dial. This factorization pins the entire spectrum to a line. Our main results are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{(Reality of Hermitian eigenvalues, abstract form.)} Any eigenvalue of a Hermitian matrix belonging to a nonzero eigenvector is real.
\item \textbf{(Spectral line-locking.)} For a symmetric edge relation and $z \ne 0$, every eigenvalue of $A = zB$ has the form $z\mu$ with $\mu \in \R$; the spectrum lies on $\R\cdot z$.
\item \textbf{(Global invariants.)} $\det(A) = z^{n}\det(B)$, and $\tr(A) = 0$ for loopless relations.
\item \textbf{(Complete-graph outlier.)} The all-ones vector is an eigenvector of the complete loopless realization with eigenvalue $(n-1)z$, and $|(n-1)z| > \sqrt{n}\,|z|$ for every $n \ge 3$, sharply beginning at $n=3$.
\end{enumerate}
The results hold \emph{exactly} for finite graphs, not merely asymptotically.

\section{Definitions and setup}

Throughout, $V$ is a finite vertex set with $n = |V|$, and matrices are indexed by $V \times V$.

\begin{definition}[Edge relation]
An \emph{edge relation} is a function $g : V \times V \to \{\text{true},\text{false}\}$. It is \emph{symmetric} (undirected) if $g(i,j) = g(j,i)$ for all $i,j$, and \emph{loopless} if $g(i,i) = \text{false}$ for all $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Indicator matrix]
The \emph{indicator matrix} $B = \ind(g)$ has $B_{ij} = 1$ if $g(i,j)$ is true and $B_{ij} = 0$ otherwise. If $g$ is symmetric then $B$ is real symmetric, hence Hermitian over $\C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fixed-amplitude adjacency matrix]
Given $z \in \C$ and an edge relation $g$, the \emph{fixed-amplitude adjacency matrix} $A = \adj(z,g)$ has $A_{ij} = z$ if $g(i,j)$ is true and $A_{ij} = 0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Scalar factorization]\label{lem:factor}
For all $z$ and $g$, $A = z\,B$, i.e.\ $\adj(z,g) = z\cdot\ind(g)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Entrywise: if $g(i,j)$ is true then $A_{ij}=z=z\cdot 1=zB_{ij}$; otherwise $A_{ij}=0=z\cdot 0=zB_{ij}$.
\end{proof}

Two consequences are used below: if $g$ is symmetric then $B$ is Hermitian; and if $Av=\lambda v$ with $z\ne 0$, then $Bv=(\lambda/z)v$ (\emph{eigenpair pullback}), since $zBv=Av=\lambda v$.

\begin{definition}[Eigenpair]
A pair $(\lambda,v)$ with $v\ne 0$ and $Av=\lambda v$ is an \emph{eigenpair}. We work with abstract eigenpairs: any nonzero solution of $Av=\lambda v$, not only those from a spectral decomposition.
\end{definition}

\section{Reality of Hermitian eigenvalues}

\begin{theorem}[Reality of Hermitian eigenvalues]\label{thm:real}
Let $A$ be Hermitian ($A^{*}=A$). If $(\mu,v)$ is an eigenpair with $v\ne 0$, then $\overline{\mu}=\mu$, i.e.\ $\mu\in\R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consider the Rayleigh scalar $Q = v^{*}Av$. Using $Av=\mu v$,
\[
Q = \mu\,(v^{*}v), \qquad c := v^{*}v = \sum_i |v_i|^2 > 0,
\]
so $c$ is a nonzero real. On the other hand, from $\overline{a\cdot b}=\overline a\cdot\overline b$, $\overline{(Av)}=\overline A\,\overline v$, and $A^{*}=A$, one computes $\overline{Q}=v^{*}Av=Q$, so $Q\in\R$. Therefore $\mu c=Q=\overline Q=\overline\mu\,\overline c=\overline\mu\, c$; cancelling the nonzero real $c$ gives $\overline\mu=\mu$.
\end{proof}

The proof uses only conjugate-symmetry of the Hermitian form, positivity of $v^{*}v$, and $A^{*}=A$; no basis or diagonalization. Hence it applies to abstract eigenpairs.

\section{Spectral line-locking}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral line-locking]\label{thm:lock}
Let $g$ be symmetric, $z\in\C$ with $z\ne 0$, and $A=\adj(z,g)$. If $(\lambda,v)$ is an eigenpair of $A$, then there is $\mu\in\R$ with
\[
\lambda = z\,\mu.
\]
Hence the spectrum of $A$ lies in the complex line $\R\cdot z=\{tz: t\in\R\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:factor}, $A=zB$ with $B=\ind(g)$ Hermitian (as $g$ is symmetric). From $Av=\lambda v$ and $z\ne 0$, the pullback gives $Bv=(\lambda/z)v$, an eigenpair of the Hermitian $B$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:real}, $\lambda/z\in\R$, so $\Imag(\lambda/z)=0$. Set $\mu:=\Real(\lambda/z)$; then $\lambda/z=\mu$ and $\lambda=z\mu$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No circular law]
For the fixed-amplitude undirected model, no rescaling of $A$ has an empirical spectral distribution converging to a rotationally invariant law on a two-dimensional disk; any nondegenerate limiting distribution is supported on $\R\cdot z$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Both hypotheses are load-bearing]
The hypothesis $z\ne 0$ is required (at $z=0$ the matrix is zero and the line's direction is undefined). Symmetry is required: without it $B$ is not Hermitian, its eigenvalues need not be real, and the products $z\mu$ leave the line.
\end{remark}

\section{Global multiplicative invariants}

\begin{theorem}[Determinant scaling]
For all $z$ and $g$ on $n=|V|$ vertices, $\det(A)=z^{\,n}\det(B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:factor}, $A=zB$; homogeneity of the determinant gives $\det(zB)=z^{n}\det(B)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing loopless trace]
If $g$ is loopless, then $\tr(A)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\tr(A)=\sum_i A_{ii}$, and each $A_{ii}=0$ because $g(i,i)$ is false.
\end{proof}

With line-locking, the eigenvalues $z\mu_1,\dots,z\mu_n$ (the $\mu_k$ real) satisfy $\sum_k z\mu_k=\tr(A)=0$ in the loopless case and $\prod_k z\mu_k=\det(A)=z^n\det(B)$.

\section{The complete-graph outlier at every order}

Let $K_n$ be the complete loopless relation on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$: $g(i,j)$ true iff $i\ne j$.

\begin{theorem}[Mean-direction eigenpair]
For $A=\adj(z,K_n)$, the all-ones vector $\one$ satisfies $A\,\one=\big((n-1)z\big)\,\one$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each row $i$, $(A\one)_i=\sum_{j\ne i} z=(n-1)z$, independent of $i$.
\end{proof}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:lock}, $(n-1)z$ lies on $\R\cdot z$ with real coordinate $\mu=n-1$: the mean-direction outlier, in which all vertices oscillate in phase.

\begin{theorem}[Outlier escapes the heuristic disk]
Let $z\ne 0$. For every $n\ge 3$,
\[
\big|(n-1)z\big| > \sqrt{n}\,|z|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $|z|>0$ and $n-1>0$, $|(n-1)z|=(n-1)|z|$; dividing by $|z|$ reduces the claim to $(n-1)>\sqrt n$, i.e.\ $(n-1)^2>n$, i.e.\ $n^2-3n+1>0$. At $n=3$ this is $1>0$, and the quadratic increases for $n\ge 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness at $n=3$]
At $n=2$, $(n-1)|z|=|z|<\sqrt{2}\,|z|$, so the eigenvalue $z$ lies strictly inside the radius. The outlier phenomenon begins exactly at $n=3$; the classical four-vertex example is representative, not special.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion}

The results assemble into one principle: \emph{fixed-amplitude complex weighting is spectrally one-dimensional.} A single shared complex scalar can only rotate and dilate a real Hermitian spectrum; it can never manufacture the two-dimensional spread of a circular law. The obstruction lives precisely in the scalar--Hermitian factorization $A=zB$: all randomness sits inside the Hermitian $B$ whose eigenvalues are pinned to $\R$, and $z$ applies a rigid rotation-dilation. Randomness in the edge set only reshuffles $B$ within the Hermitian class.

This isolates the hypotheses whose violation is necessary for disk-filling spectra: breaking symmetry (directed edges, making $B$ non-Hermitian) or breaking the shared phase (independent per-edge phases, destroying $A=zB$). Line-locking tells us where \emph{not} to look and thereby aims the search for two-dimensional spectra at the models that violate the factorization.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{(1) Two-dimensional spectra require broken phase symmetry.} If each present edge carries an independent unit-modulus weight (relaxing $w_{ji}=\overline{w_{ij}}$ toward independence), the empirical spectral distribution should converge to a rotationally invariant law on a full disk---the sharp counterpoint to line-locking.

\textbf{(2) A phase-transition threshold for the mean-direction outlier.} For $G(n,p)$ with weight $z$, there should be a sharp $p^{*}(n)$ above which the largest-modulus eigenvalue is asymptotically $p(n-1)|z|$ while the bulk stays within $2\sqrt{np(1-p)}\,|z|$; separation occurs when $p(n-1)>2\sqrt{np(1-p)}$.

\textbf{(3) Reality of Rayleigh quotients characterizes the line-locked class.} The line-locked weighting schemes should be characterized precisely by reality of their Rayleigh quotients, i.e.\ by an underlying Hermitian core.

\section{Conclusion}

The fixed-amplitude complex-weighted undirected random graph has a spectrum rigidly locked to a single line through the origin---an exact, finite phenomenon driven by an elementary reality theorem for Hermitian Rayleigh quotients, complemented by clean global invariants and a sharp complete-graph outlier bound. The analysis pins down the precise algebraic obstruction and charts where genuine two-dimensional spectra must be sought.

\end{document}

algorithms.pseudocode:
function TRANSPORT_SPECTRUM(z, edges):
    B <- real matrix with B[i,j] = 1 if edges[i,j] else 0
    mu[1..n] <- eigenvalues of the real symmetric matrix B   # O(n^3)
    for k in 1..n:
        lambda[k] <- z * mu[k]                                # O(1) each
    return lambda[1..n]

function COMPLETE_OUTLIER(z, n):
    eig    <- (n - 1) * z          # exact eigenvalue, all-ones eigenvector
    modulus<- |eig|
    radius <- sqrt(n) * |z|
    escapes<- (modulus > radius)   # true iff n >= 3
    return (eig, modulus, radius, escapes)

