# Erdős–Rényi on Acid: Random Graphs That Refuse to Hallucinate

Flip a coin for every possible friendship among $n$ people and you get the famous **Erdős–Rényi random graph**. But what happens when the *weight* of a friendship is not a yes/no coin but a **complex number** $z = re^{i\theta}$ — a length and a direction on the clock face of the complex plane? You might expect chaos: a spectrum smeared across a two-dimensional disk, like static on a screen. The truth is the opposite, and it is beautiful.

Every present edge sharing the same amplitude $z$ makes the weighted adjacency matrix factor as
$$A = z\cdot B,$$
where $B$ is the plain real, symmetric, zero-one graph matrix. This single fact drives everything below.

## The one-line theorem

Because $B$ is symmetric, all of its eigenvalues are **real** — a fact you can feel by dragging the sliders below. Multiplying by $z$ can only rotate and stretch that real line. So the whole spectrum locks onto the single complex line $\mathbb{R}\cdot z$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the formal statement and proof sketch</summary>

**Line-Locking Theorem.** For a symmetric edge relation and nonzero amplitude $z$, every eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $A=zB$ satisfies $\lambda = z\mu$ for some real $\mu$.

*Proof.* Write $A = zB$ with $B$ Hermitian. If $Av=\lambda v$ with $v\neq 0$ and $z\neq 0$, then $Bv=(\lambda/z)v$. The Rayleigh scalar $v^{*}Bv$ equals both $(\lambda/z)\sum_i|v_i|^2$ and its own conjugate (because $B^{*}=B$), so it is real; dividing by the positive real $\sum_i|v_i|^2$ forces $\lambda/z$ real. Hence $\lambda = z\mu$ with $\mu=\operatorname{Re}(\lambda/z)$. $\blacksquare$
</details>

The engine is a classic **Rayleigh-quotient** argument, applied to *any* eigenpair — no diagonalization needed.

{{algorithm:1}}

## The loud collective mode

Not every point on the line is equal. On the **complete graph**, where everyone knows everyone, the democratic all-ones mode hums with eigenvalue $(n-1)z$ — and it flees far past the heuristic radius $\sqrt{n}\,|z|$. Slide $n$ below and watch the badge flip exactly at $n=3$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why the threshold is exactly n = 3</summary>
The escape condition $|(n-1)z| > \sqrt{n}\,|z|$ reduces to $(n-1)^2 > n$, i.e. $n^2-3n+1>0$. At $n=3$ this is $1>0$; at $n=2$ it is $-1<0$. So the phenomenon begins sharply at three vertices, and the classical four-vertex example is representative, not special.
</details>

