## The takeaway

Give a random graph a single shared voice, and no matter how wildly its connections flicker, it will always sing on one note. The value of the line-locking theorem is that, by pinning down *exactly* what forces one-dimensionality — the scalar–Hermitian factorization — it tells future explorers precisely where the two-dimensional chaos must hide: not in the amplitude, but in the phases. For more on the classical backdrop, see the [circular law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circular_law) and [Wigner's semicircle law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wigner_semicircle_distribution).
